  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nOverall a quiet early morning and noticeably warmer than what it was \n24 hours ago at this time. Current temperatures range in the mid to \nupper 50s across most areas with mostly clear skies. A warm front \ncurrently spans from central North Dakota down to central Iowa. \nAloft, troughing over northern Manitoba will move east into \nOntario today pulling this surface warm front farther into MN. \nThis warm advective pattern will support breezy conditions with\nwind speeds between 20 to 30 mph for at least the first half of\ntoday. Winds will decrease through the course of the afternoon \nas the front pushes farther to the northeast. The result of this\nis temperatures are forecast to reach the upper 70s for most \ntoday while even a few areas southwest of the MN River Valley\ncould see the lower 80s.\n\nBy Wednesday, the aforementioned troughing pattern deepens over\nthe Hudson Bay region. On the western side of this trough, a \nsurface cold front will help reduce temperatures slightly back \ninto the lower 70 to mid 70s. No rain is expected with this \nfront but drier air, breezy conditions, and warmer temps will \nhelp elevate fire weather concerns across portions of western \nMN. Thursday through this weekend remains dry as upper-level \nridging becomes the dominant feature. Temperatures will \ngradually climb back into the upper 70s once again by Saturday \nbefore falling to slightly below normal temperatures early next \nweek. Still lots of modeling time between now and early next \nweek but potentially something tries to get going near the Four\nCoroners region Monday into Tuesday. Once this front passes, \ntemepratures will cool to slightly below- normal in the upper \n50s.",
